<section xml:id="sec_circuit-intro2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>What Is a Circuit?</title>

	<outcomes xml:id="adapting-model-circuits">
	<title>Electric Circuits</title>
	<ul>
		<li>
		<p>You will now apply what you have learned about electric fields and electric potential to the broadly applicable study of <alert>electric circuits</alert>.</p>
	</li>
</ul>
</outcomes>
	

		<activity>
      <title>Warm-up: Everyday Circuits</title>
      <p></p>
			  <p>Write down at least one thing you already know about electric circuits from your previous coursework and/or your everyday experiences.</p>
		</activity>

<corollary xml:id="vid-circuits">
	<title>Electric Circuits</title>
	
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>

		<video youtube="PxVLDZpSjWk"><description>Introducing electric circuits.</description></video>

	</statement>
  </corollary>

			<subsubsection>
		<title>Key Ideas</title>
	</subsubsection>

	<definition>
		<title>Circuit</title>
		<statement>
			<p></p>
			<p>A <term>circuit</term> is a continuous path of electrical conductors.</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>A circuit is made of conducting elements.</alert>  Any object that is electrically conductive can be a circuit element.  Common elements include wires, batteries, light bulbs, resistors, capacitors, and switches.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>An electric field will force current to flow.</alert>  For current to flow through the conductors that make up a circuit, an electric field must be present <em>inside the conductors</em>, when they are not in electrostatic equilibrium.  Such an electric field is most commonly caused by including a battery in the circuit.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>Current can flow through a complete circuit.</alert>  A circuit is only complete if there is at least one continuous path of electrical conductors and circuit elements that starts and ends at the same location.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>Current cannot flow through a broken circuit.</alert>  If a circuit is <em>broken</em> by a nonconductive element, such as an insulator or an open switch, no current will flow.  It only takes one break in a circuit to stop current from flowing.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>Every circuit element has a voltage across it.</alert>  The voltage is measured from one side of the element to the other and is usually represented as <m>\Delta V_{\text{element}}</m> or sometimes even just <m>V_{\text{element}}</m>.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>Every circuit element has a current through it.</alert>  The current is measured as the amount of positive charge passing through the element per unit time and is usually represented as <m>I_{\text{element}}</m>.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ul>

			<figure xml:id="precircuit">
			  <title>Circuit Figure</title>
			<caption><alert>Example:</alert> A simple circuit consisting of three unknown circuit elements (A, B, and C).</caption>
			<image source="circ/PreCircuit.png" width = "50%" ><description>A diagram of a complete circuit consisting of a square marked with three boxes (A, B, and C) on the left, upper, and right sides, respectively. A small dot below box A is marked as a starting point.</description></image>
		</figure>
		</statement>
	</definition>
	
	<activity xml:id="going-around-a-circuit">
      <title>Going around a Circuit</title>
	  <introduction>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>Imagine that you are a tiny positive charge, initially located at the dot in the <xref ref="precircuit"/> above.  You begin moving clockwise through the circuit. As you pass through element A, you measure a gain in voltage of <m>\Delta V_A = 5 \mathrm{~V}</m> and you measure a current of <m>I_A = 0.7 \mathrm{~A}</m>. As you pass through element B, you measure a voltage decrease of <m>\Delta V_B = -3 \mathrm{~V}</m>.</p>
	  </introduction>
	  <task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				How do you think the voltage will change as you pass through element C?  Explain your reasoning.
			</p>
		</statement>
	  </task>
	  <task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				What do you think is the current through element B? Through element C?  Explain your reasoning.
			</p>
		</statement>
	  </task>			
		</activity>

		<conjecture xml:id="rep_circuit-diagrams">
		<title>Circuit Diagrams</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>
				A <term>circuit diagram</term> is a simplified representation of a real circuit.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>Each element is given a simple icon.</alert>  The most common items are collected in the figure below.
					</p>
					<figure xml:id="CD_First">
			  <title>Circuit Elements Diagram</title>
			<caption><alert>Example</alert> circuit elements in circuit diagram notation.</caption>
			<image source="CD_All.png" width = "100%" ><description>Seven circuit elements, from left to right: a wire (a straight line), a battery (two horizontal lines of different length), a light bulb (a circle around an ellipse), a junction (a horizontal line meeting a vertical line at the middle), a switch (a diagonal line opposite an open circle), a resistor (a jagged line), and a capacitor (two parallel lines of equal length).</description></image>
		</figure>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>Circuit diagrams show electrical connections.</alert>  The physical arrangement of the circuit elements does not matter: only how they are electrically connected.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>Ideal wires are represented with straight lines.</alert>  Circuit elements connected by ideal wires are assumed to be electrically connected.
					</p>
				</li>
				
			</ul>
			<figure xml:id="circuit-diagram1">
			  <title>Example Circuit Diagram</title>
			<caption><alert>Example:</alert> two circuit diagrams.  Although they look slightly different, the electrical connections are the same, so they represent the same physical circuit.</caption>
			<image source="circuit-diagram1.png" width = "65%" ><description>Two circuit diagrams.  Left: a battery on the left branch and two light bulbs on the right branch.  Right: a battery on the left branch, a bulb on the top, and another bulb on the right.</description></image>
		</figure>

					
		</statement>
	</conjecture>	


<!--NO AI-->
